Q: Why do register_tm_clones and deregister_tm_clones get tacked on to simple programs? A: <p>Those functions are <a href="https://csshub.pk/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">normal</a> GCC runtime scaffolding and are not being generated because your program explicitly uses transactional memory.</p>
<p><code>register_tm_clones()</code> and <code>deregister_tm_clones()</code> come from GCC's CRT startup code (<code>crtstuff.c</code>). They support GCC's transactional-memory clone registration mechanism.</p>
<p>GCC-generated startup objects are linked into normal executables automatically. The same generic startup code is used whether or not the particular program ends up having transactional-memory clones.</p>
<p>In a program such as:</p>
<pre><code>int main() {
    return 123;
}
</code></pre>
<p>there are no TM clones to register, so the generated registration functions effectively become no-ops. That is why a decompiler may show something equivalent to:</p>
<pre><code>void register_tm_clones() { return; }
void deregister_tm_clones() { return; }
</code></pre>
<p>This also does <strong>not necessarily mean that <code>libitm</code> is actually being used by your program</strong>. The CRT code contains support for the mechanism and can reference the <code>_ITM_registerTMCloneTable</code> / <code>_ITM_deregisterTMCloneTable</code> interfaces when they are available.</p>
<p>In short, these functions are generic compiler/runtime bookkeeping. They aren't needed by your <code>main()</code> specifically, but including a tiny amount of common startup infrastructure is simpler than generating completely different CRT code for every executable.</p>
